<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1486">1486</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sri Chaitanya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu was a Bengali saint and the inspiration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavism</a>, a devotional movement centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Radha. Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabadwip">Nabadwip</a> in 1486, he spread congregational chanting of Krishna's names and spent his later years at Puri, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> temple, where the Gajapati king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-deva">Prataparudra Deva</a> became his devotee. His followers, among them the Goswamis of Vrindavan, gave the movement its theology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu.jpg" alt="Chaitanya Mahaprabhu" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaitanya_dances_with_followers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
